  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T06:01:59.183Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSection 51 of the NIRC requires individuals to file an income tax return, with specific deadlines depending on their income sources and circumstances. Generally, individuals must file their returns on or before April 15 of each year for the preceding taxable year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe controlling authority for this requirement is Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51 outlines the requirements for filing income tax returns, specifying that every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, as well as those residing abroad on income sourced within the Philippines, must file a return. Additionally, individuals subject to capital gains tax must file returns within 30 days of each transaction and a consolidated return by April 15.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis provision ensures that individuals report their income and pay the appropriate taxes to the government. The April 15 deadline is crucial for compliance, and failure to file on time may result in penalties. Understanding these requirements helps taxpayers avoid issues with the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) and ensures proper tax compliance.",
